export const priorityGuideContent = {
  "hacoo-finds-category-budget": {
    kicker: "Hacoo finds / category budget",
    dateLabel: `Published and reviewed ${reviewDate}`,
    intro: "A useful Hacoo finds budget is not a single amount spread across everything that looks interesting. It is a category plan tied to a wardrobe problem, the number of times an item could realistically be worn and the amount of uncertainty still present in the listing. Setting those limits before browsing changes the task from collecting attractive possibilities to choosing one role at a time. This guide shows how to divide attention as carefully as money, so a shortlist remains useful even when prices, options or availability change on the live destination.",
    factBox: [["Start with", "One wardrobe gap and one category"], ["Budget includes", "Item limit, uncertainty allowance and a stop rule"], ["Final check", "Current option, measurements and destination terms"]],
    sections: [
      ["Define the wardrobe job before the amount", [
        "Begin with a sentence that describes use: a light layer for cool commutes, two plain tops for a weekly rotation, or shoes for long indoor days. The sentence should name an occasion and an existing outfit. That prevents a budget from becoming permission to browse every category. If the need cannot be connected to something you already wear, keep it on an inspiration list rather than giving it purchasing priority. A wardrobe job is specific enough to test; a mood such as wanting something new is not.",
        "Next, identify what would make the item fail. A jacket may be useless if it cannot close over your usual hoodie. Shoes may fail if the internal-length method is unclear. A bag may fail if it cannot hold the object that motivated the search. These conditions belong beside the budget because an inexpensive item that misses its job still consumes space and attention. The first budget decision is therefore not how much to spend, but what evidence must exist before any candidate deserves comparison."
      ]],
      ["Divide the plan by category priority", [
        "Use three priority levels. A need category solves a repeated problem and can receive most of the available attention. A support category improves outfits that already work and receives a smaller allowance. An experiment category is optional and should have the strictest item limit. This division keeps eight open tabs from pretending to be eight equal needs. A missing everyday layer deserves a different decision process from a novelty accessory, even if the accessory is cheaper or more visually memorable.",
        "Write the planned number of items beside each category before recording candidates. One jacket slot means that two jacket listings compete for the same role; it does not create permission to keep both. If a better candidate appears, it replaces the weaker one. The item cap is valuable because prices can shift and sales language can make an original amount feel negotiable. A category cap stays understandable: the wardrobe still needs one layer, not three merely because the page changed."
      ]],
      ["Price uncertainty separately from product value", [
        "A listing price is only one visible figure and can change with the selected option. The cheapest displayed option may not be the size, colour or version you examined. Record the exact option beside the observed amount and treat it as a dated snapshot, never a promise. Do not build a plan that works only if every provisional number remains unchanged. Leave room for differences that become visible at the current destination, while avoiding invented estimates for costs the page has not confirmed.",
        "Uncertainty also has a non-financial cost. Missing measurements, conflicting photographs and unclear material information require more review and increase the chance of a poor match. Give every candidate a simple evidence status: comparable, incomplete or unresolved. A comparable option may justify more of the category budget because its fit and role can be evaluated. An unresolved option should not receive extra allowance simply because the cover image is appealing. Money should follow decision quality, not compensate for missing information."
      ]],
      ["Use cost per realistic wear as a filter", [
        "You do not need to predict an exact lifetime to use wear frequency intelligently. Estimate whether the item belongs in a weekly, monthly, seasonal or rare rotation. Then name at least three existing outfits and two real occasions for it. If that is difficult, the item is probably serving inspiration rather than a wardrobe gap. A slightly higher category allocation can be rational for something worn repeatedly; a low price does not make a once-only experiment more useful.",
        "Keep the calculation qualitative when the evidence is weak. Instead of claiming a precise cost per wear, label candidates high, medium or low expected rotation and explain why. A neutral hoodie that works with four bottoms may have high coverage. A jacket that duplicates the warmth, colour and silhouette of one you own may have low incremental value. This method avoids false precision while still forcing the key comparison: what new work will this item perform in the wardrobe?"
      ]],
      ["Create a stop rule that survives browsing", [
        "A stop rule closes the session when the planned job is filled, when every remaining option lacks decisive information or when the shortlist exceeds three candidates. It should also require a cooling-off review. Revisit the list after the initial visual excitement has faded and remove anything that cannot be paired with existing clothes. The goal is not to punish interest; it is to see whether the original wardrobe sentence still explains the choice without help from a promotional image.",
        "During the review, compare the strongest candidate with doing nothing. If the current wardrobe can already handle the occasion, the correct use of the budget may be to preserve it. If the gap remains real, compare only candidates that pass the measurement and option checks. Do not move unused allowance into an unrelated category automatically. Unspent budget is evidence that the plan worked, not a failure to complete a shopping list."
      ]],
      ["Turn the final shortlist into a decision record", [
        "For each finalist, save the category, exact option, current link, observed amount, decisive measurements, expected rotation and one unresolved point. Add the date because listings move. Keep the record short enough to review on a phone. A strong row might say that a layer fits the planned chest range, works with three owned bottoms and still lacks a clear lining description. That is more useful than a generic score because it preserves the reason for the decision.",
        "Open the current destination only after the comparison fields are ready. Confirm that the option has not reset, that the live amount belongs to it and that the relevant terms are visible. If a decisive detail changed, return the candidate to unresolved rather than stretching the budget logic to protect an earlier preference. A category budget succeeds when it makes the final check calmer, smaller and easier to explain—not when it guarantees that every planned slot gets filled.",
        "Review the record as one wardrobe plan, not eight separate bargains. Check whether several categories depend on the same future purchase, whether the same neutral color is being added repeatedly and whether care requirements compete for limited time. If one candidate is removed, do not immediately replace it. Reconfirm the original gap and give the remaining allowance a named status: reserved, released or awaiting evidence. That final step keeps category planning useful after the browsing session ends."
        ,"Schedule one later review of saved candidates instead of repeated casual checking. At that review, remove unavailable routes, confirm whether the wardrobe gap still exists and compare only the fields that changed. A planned review reduces the pressure created by temporary page language while preserving the option to act when evidence improves. It also creates a clean boundary: outside the review window, new discoveries go to inspiration rather than silently expanding the active category budget."
      ]]
    ],
    steps: ["Name one wardrobe job", "Set category and item limits", "Compare only evidence-ready candidates", "Recheck the exact live option"],
    sourceNote: "Independent wardrobe-planning method reviewed against the site's current category routes on September 15, 2026. Prices and availability are intentionally treated as changeable live information."
  },
  "hacoo-clothing-color-palette": {
    kicker: "Hacoo clothing / color-palette plan",
    dateLabel: `Published and reviewed ${reviewDate}`,
    intro: "A Hacoo clothing color palette should begin with the clothes you repeatedly wear, not with a row of attractive swatches. The useful question is whether a new color connects to existing shoes, bottoms and layers under the lighting and occasions that matter to you. A small palette makes that relationship visible. It also exposes when two candidates perform the same visual role. This guide builds an anchor, support and accent system, then applies it to live product images without pretending that screens can prove an exact shade.",
    factBox: [["Anchor colors", "Two or three shades already common in your wardrobe"], ["Support colors", "Easy bridges that work with several anchors"], ["Accent limit", "One deliberate contrast role at a time"]],
    sections: [
      ["Audit what you actually wear", [
        "Choose ten recent outfits from ordinary days rather than special-event photographs. List the main colors of tops, bottoms, outerwear and shoes. Repeated shades are your real anchors, even if they are less exciting than the colors saved in an inspiration folder. An anchor earns its place by appearing across categories: dark trousers that work with several tops, a shoe color repeated through the week, or an outer layer that ties different combinations together.",
        "Separate owned colors from aspirational ones. A bright tone you admire but never wear should not be treated as a core color merely because it appears in saved Hacoo finds. Give it the accent role and test it with complete outfits. This avoids buying several pieces that coordinate with each other but not with the wardrobe already in use. The audit is not a rule against change; it is a way to see how much change one new item would require."
      ]],
      ["Build three functional color roles", [
        "Use two or three anchors, two support shades and one optional accent. Anchors carry large areas such as trousers, jackets or frequently worn shoes. Support shades bridge anchors and can appear in tees, knits or accessories. The accent provides contrast but does not need to match everything. This role system is more flexible than demanding that every color pair perfectly, because real outfits need balance and repetition rather than identical tones.",
        "Name the shades in plain, useful language—warm off-white, cool charcoal, muted olive—rather than relying on a listing's creative color name. Then note undertone and depth where they affect combinations. A warm cream can behave differently from optical white beside the same brown jacket. Exact digital color values would suggest a precision product photography cannot provide, so the goal is a workable family and contrast level, not laboratory matching."
      ]],
      ["Test every candidate with three outfit anchors", [
        "Before a color enters the shortlist, pair it with three things you own: one bottom, one shoe and one layer or bag. The combinations should represent actual use, not theoretical compatibility. If a green top works only with a pair of trousers you rarely wear, its wardrobe coverage is narrow. If it works with denim, black trousers and the jacket used most weeks, it has a clearer role even if the shade is less dramatic in isolation.",
        "Photograph the owned anchors together in neutral daylight if possible. This creates a personal reference board that is more dependable than memory. Keep screens at normal brightness and compare multiple listing images, including less edited views when available. A candidate passes when the color family and contrast appear workable across the set. It does not pass because one promotional image happens to resemble one saved outfit."
      ]],
      ["Allow for the limits of product images", [
        "Lighting, white balance, display settings, image compression and fabric texture all affect perceived color. Velvet, brushed fleece and glossy nylon can appear lighter or darker as the angle changes. Treat a single cover image as inspiration only. Look for several views with a consistent background, and check whether customer or inspection images show the same general family. Conflicting images should be marked unresolved instead of averaged into the shade you hope to receive.",
        "Color names are also weak evidence. Beige can describe pink, yellow, grey or brown undertones, and navy can range from nearly black to visibly blue. If the distinction would decide whether the item works with your anchors, make it a stop condition. Choose a candidate with clearer evidence or accept that the color is an experiment and limit its budget and wardrobe role accordingly. Honest uncertainty prevents a palette from becoming a false promise."
      ]],
      ["Control duplicates by role, not by shade name", [
        "Two garments do not need identical labels to duplicate each other. A stone hoodie and an oatmeal sweater may both occupy the same light-neutral casual-layer role. Compare silhouette, warmth, occasion and outfit coverage alongside color. If one adds no new combination or seasonal function, the difference in shade may not justify both. Conversely, two black items can serve clearly different roles when one is a formal trouser and the other a weatherproof outer layer.",
        "Use a small matrix with candidates as rows and anchor outfits as columns. Mark works, uncertain or conflicts. Add a separate role column: base, bridge or accent. The best addition is usually the option that creates several believable outfits without forcing more purchases. Avoid ranking colors as universally best. The useful conclusion is personal and conditional: this shade supports these owned pieces in these situations with this degree of visual confidence."
      ]],
      ["Recheck the exact option before acting", [
        "Color selectors can use thumbnails that differ from the main gallery, and changing size may reset the selected color. Save the exact option text and a dated screenshot that includes the selector. On the live destination, confirm the selection again, then review measurements and construction. A color match cannot rescue the wrong fit, and a perfect silhouette cannot make uncertain color evidence disappear when coordination is the main purpose.",
        "After the item arrives, compare it with the same anchor group in daylight and indoor light before changing the palette plan. Record which combinations work rather than declaring the online image accurate or inaccurate in every setting. This feedback improves future shortlists because it reveals your tolerance for undertone and contrast. A useful Hacoo color palette remains a living wardrobe tool, not a shopping theme that requires every saved find to be purchased.",
        "Keep seasonal changes separate from permanent anchors. A deep accent that works with winter outerwear may disappear from warm-weather outfits, while a support neutral can bridge both seasons. Mark each candidate all-year, warm-season or cool-season and count only the outfits available in that period. This prevents a theoretically versatile shade from receiving credit for combinations that would never be worn at the same time.",
        "When two options appear close, compare contrast rather than chasing an exact match. Ask whether the new item should blend with the anchor, create a medium separation or become the focal point. That decision remains valid even if photography shifts the perceived shade slightly. If only an exact match would work, the online evidence may be insufficient; choose an intentional contrast or wait for a more reliable view."
        ,"Keep metal hardware, prints and trims in the palette review when they occupy visible space. A bag may be brown overall yet introduce bright hardware that conflicts with the quiet role you planned; a black jacket may carry a large graphic that behaves like an accent. Record the whole visible composition rather than assigning one color label. This is especially important when the listing selector changes trim or print together with the base shade."
      ]]
    ],
    steps: ["Identify worn anchor colors", "Assign support and accent roles", "Test three owned-outfit combinations", "Confirm the exact live color option"],
    sourceNote: "Independent wardrobe and image-evidence method. Current category imagery was reviewed September 15, 2026; no screen image is treated as proof of exact color."
  },
  "hacoo-shoes-everyday-use": {
    kicker: "Hacoo shoes / everyday-use matrix",
    dateLabel: `Published and reviewed ${reviewDate}`,
    intro: "The strongest Hacoo shoes shortlist begins with the hours, surfaces and outfits the pair must handle. Everyday use is not a style category: it may mean a standing commute, indoor study, short errands, wet pavements or a mixed day. Each context changes which evidence matters. This guide turns that context into a compact fit and use-case matrix. It cannot guarantee comfort from photographs, but it can prevent appearance from hiding missing length, width, sole and rotation information.",
    factBox: [["Reference", "A comfortable pair used for a similar day"], ["Decisive evidence", "Length method, width context, upper and sole construction"], ["Shortlist size", "Two or three pairs for one defined role"]],
    sections: [
      ["Describe the real day the shoes must cover", [
        "Write a simple timeline: twenty minutes walking, six hours mostly indoors, stairs, another walk home. Add surface, weather and whether you carry weight. This is more useful than the label everyday because it identifies pressure points. Long standing makes cushioning and stable fit more important; wet routes make outsole pattern and upper care relevant; frequent removal may change the preferred closure. The shoe should be evaluated for the hardest regular part of the day, not the easiest photograph.",
        "Name the role in your current rotation. Are you replacing a worn pair, adding a weather option or filling a gap between athletic and smarter shoes? If three owned pairs already cover the same days, a new candidate must offer a clear functional difference. Avoid inventing a performance advantage from design alone. A thick-looking sole does not prove cushioning, and a textured outsole image does not prove grip on every surface. Record visible construction separately from expected use."
      ]],
      ["Build the fit reference before reading a size chart", [
        "Measure both feet at the end of a normal day with the socks you plan to wear. Record the longer foot. If possible, remove and measure the insole from a comfortable shoe with a similar toe shape. Also note where that reference feels roomy or tight. Foot length, insole length and outsole length are different measurements; a useful listing or chart should identify which one it uses before any size conversion is trusted.",
        "Width and volume can be as decisive as length. Note whether your forefoot is constrained in tapered shoes, whether the heel slips in wide collars and whether an orthotic requires extra depth. Product photographs may show shape, but perspective can distort it. Look for top views, side views and a visible measurement method. When width or internal length is essential and absent, mark the candidate unresolved. Choosing the familiar regional size number does not solve missing geometry."
      ]],
      ["Read the upper and closure as a system", [
        "The upper influences structure, flexibility, ventilation and care, but appearance alone cannot confirm a material claim. Use the current description for stated composition and photographs for visible paneling, lining and reinforcement. Ask where the foot needs movement and where it needs support during the defined day. A rigid panel at a flex point may matter more to you than decorative differences elsewhere, while a soft upper may still need a secure heel and usable closure.",
        "Examine lacing length, eyelet placement, tongue shape, collar padding and heel structure together. A closure should allow adjustment over your intended socks without bunching or leaving excessive slack. Slip-on convenience may suit short indoor use but offer less adjustability for variable swelling. These are decision questions, not promises about comfort. The listing can show components; only a properly matched fit and real wear can establish how the system feels."
      ]],
      ["Inspect the sole without overclaiming performance", [
        "Use side images to understand stack and flex zones, rear images to check alignment, and outsole images to see contact pattern. Look for a clear border between upper and sole, consistent joins and enough photographic detail to understand construction. Do not translate visual thickness into a measured benefit. Foam type, density, flex and durability require reliable specifications or testing that an independent product image does not provide.",
        "Match visible design to the day conservatively. A flat, broad contact area may look stable for indoor use; deep channels may appear suited to debris; exposed light-colored material may show wear quickly. Phrase each conclusion as a suitability question to verify, not a guarantee. If wet grip, impact protection or technical performance is critical, choose documented product information rather than inferring it from a Hacoo find. Everyday style research should not imitate laboratory testing."
      ]],
      ["Check outfit coverage and maintenance", [
        "Put the candidate beside photographs of three ordinary outfits, including the trousers with the narrowest and widest opening you wear. Shoe volume changes how those proportions read. A pair that works only with one special combination has a different wardrobe value from one that covers work, errands and casual evenings. Color matters, but silhouette, sole volume and trouser break often determine whether a pair actually enters rotation.",
        "Include care reality. Light textiles, brushed materials, open mesh and complex paneling may require different maintenance. Do not assume the material from a photograph; record what is stated and what remains unknown. If you avoid frequent cleaning, an everyday pair that depends on it may be a poor match regardless of price. The best shortlist reflects the wearer you are, not an ideal routine imagined during browsing."
      ]],
      ["Score evidence, not excitement", [
        "Use columns for day fit, measurement clarity, width evidence, closure, sole evidence, outfit coverage and unresolved issues. Give each field pass, uncertain or fail instead of a numeric total that can hide one decisive problem. A shoe with six passes and an unknown internal length may still be unsuitable. A visually quieter option with complete measurements may be the stronger everyday candidate because its uncertainty is smaller and its role is clearer.",
        "Finish on the live option. Confirm color and size, match the chart to that option and save the date. Recheck availability and current destination terms. If the page changes, rebuild only the affected evidence rather than carrying forward an old conclusion. The matrix does not promise that a shoe will feel comfortable; it shows whether the available information supports trying that exact pair for the defined everyday role.",
        "Add a replacement threshold before acting. Decide which current pair will leave the rotation, which signs make replacement necessary and whether the candidate solves that pair's known weakness. Without this threshold, a new pair can be called everyday while competing with several serviceable options. Replacement logic is especially useful when visual novelty makes frequency seem higher than it is likely to be after the first few weeks.",
        "Finally, distinguish reversible style compromises from irreversible fit risks. A lace swap or different trouser pairing may change appearance; missing internal length or unsuitable width cannot be fixed as easily. Give fit evidence priority when the two conflict. This weighting makes the matrix practical because it directs attention to the factors most likely to determine whether the shoes enter a real rotation."
        ,"Use reviews only to generate checks that apply to the exact decision. A report about sizing can prompt you to inspect the chart method, while a comment about a different model or country should not become evidence for this pair. Record the date, option and context when public feedback is specific enough to help. Keep anecdotal comfort descriptions separate from measurements and specifications, because another person's foot shape and daily route may be entirely different."
      ]]
    ],
    steps: ["Map one real day", "Measure feet and a reference pair", "Compare construction and outfit coverage", "Confirm the exact live option"],
    sourceNote: "Independent fit and use-case method based on current shoe category evidence reviewed September 15, 2026. It does not certify comfort, materials or technical performance."
  },
  "hacoo-sneakers-shortlist": {
    kicker: "Hacoo sneakers / comfort-first shortlist",
    dateLabel: `Published and reviewed ${reviewDate}`,
    intro: "Hacoo sneakers often enter a saved list because of silhouette and color, yet the decision usually succeeds or fails on information the cover image cannot show: internal space, heel hold, flex, weight, lining and the way the pair fits a real rotation. A comfort-first shortlist does not claim that photographs can predict feel. It removes candidates whose evidence is too weak for the intended use and gives visual preference a proper place after the functional checks.",
    factBox: [["First filter", "Intended wear duration and surface"], ["Fit anchor", "Foot plus insole measurements from a comparable pair"], ["Decision language", "Supported, uncertain or unsuitable for this use"]],
    sections: [
      ["Separate sneaker inspiration from sneaker need", [
        "Save inspiration freely, but create the shortlist only after naming the missing role. Perhaps the wardrobe needs a low-profile pair for wide trousers, a supportive-looking option for long indoor days or a darker pair for wet-season rotation. The role should describe use and outfit proportion, not a logo or trend. This separation keeps several visually similar sneakers from appearing meaningfully different when they would all be worn in the same narrow set of situations.",
        "Check what is already owned. Record the pair used most, the pair avoided and the physical reason for each. Heel slip, toe pressure, weight, difficult cleaning and poor trouser balance are stronger filters than a generic preference for comfort. The new shortlist should preserve what works and address one known problem. Without this baseline, product research becomes a contest between images rather than an improvement on lived experience."
      ]],
      ["Create a comfort evidence sheet", [
        "Use fields that a listing can reasonably support: chart method, top-view shape, visible collar and tongue padding, closure adjustability, lining, insole information, sole construction and total weight if stated. Keep a separate column for what cannot be known before wear, including pressure points, heat over time and cushioning feel. This prevents descriptive words such as soft or lightweight from turning into conclusions without a measurement, material specification or credible context.",
        "Photographs are strongest when angles are comparable. A top view can show taper; a rear view can show collar and heel geometry; a side view can show sole profile; a clear interior image can reveal seams and insole shape. None proves comfort, but together they identify questions. If every image is angled for drama and the chart method is absent, the correct status is uncertain, even when the sneaker is visually the favorite."
      ]],
      ["Match size evidence to one exact option", [
        "Record foot length and a comfortable insole length in centimetres. Note the reference pair's toe shape and sock thickness. Then identify whether the candidate chart uses foot, insole or another measurement. Do not apply a standard allowance blindly or convert through several regional systems. If the option selector and chart use different labels, preserve both and require a clear mapping before the pair enters the final shortlist.",
        "Use the longer foot and consider width, instep and heel volume. A broad-looking toe box in a perspective image is not a width measurement. If your fit depends on a wide forefoot, orthotic depth or narrow heel, treat missing evidence as decisive. It is better to keep one well-documented candidate than three familiar size labels supported by guesswork. The shortlist is a decision tool, not a collection target."
      ]],
      ["Compare silhouette with the clothes you own", [
        "Sneaker volume affects the whole outfit. Photograph candidate silhouettes beside your most-used straight, wide and tapered bottoms. Check sole thickness, toe shape, color blocking and the gap or break where fabric meets the shoe. A bulky pair may balance wide trousers and overwhelm narrow hems; a low-profile pair may do the reverse. These are styling relationships, not universal rules, so test them against your own proportions.",
        "Demand at least three credible outfits and two occasions. If a sneaker requires buying new trousers or an entirely new color palette, note that dependency. It may still be a deliberate experiment, but it should not be scored as a versatile everyday option. The strongest candidate is not always the most neutral; it is the one whose role, outfits and expected frequency are clear without inventing future wardrobe purchases."
      ]],
      ["Account for care and rotation", [
        "Everyday sneakers need recovery time and maintenance that fit your routine. Review visible material zones, light colors, deep texture, mesh openings and complex overlays. Use only stated material information, and avoid turning appearance into a durability claim. Ask whether the pair can be cleaned with methods you already use and whether weather exposure would reduce its practical rotation. A great dry-day option should be labeled that way rather than expected to serve every condition.",
        "Rotation also changes value. If the candidate duplicates a pair that is already reliable, explain what improves: darker color, easier maintenance, different profile or a better match for one outfit family. If nothing improves, the new pair competes with a proven shoe and may rarely be chosen. Comfort-first planning includes the comfort of the whole wardrobe system—easy choices, realistic care and a clear reason to reach for the pair."
      ]],
      ["Make the final comparison conditional", [
        "Compare no more than three pairs using pass, pause or remove. Pass means the available evidence supports the intended role. Pause means one answer—often measurement method or exact option identity—is still needed. Remove means a decisive requirement conflicts with the evidence. Avoid averaging fields into a star score because one missing fit measurement can matter more than several appealing details. Write the condition that would move a paused candidate forward.",
        "Before leaving the guide, open the current destination and confirm the selected size, color, images, chart and current terms. Save a dated record because the route may change. Keep the conclusion narrow: this option has enough evidence to consider for this use. That wording is more honest than promising comfort, and it produces a shortlist that can be rechecked later without recreating the entire research session.",
        "Run a final conflict check between the evidence sheet and the inspiration that started the search. If the favorite image shows a different color, lacing, trouser width or camera angle from the exact option, remove it from the decision record. Compare only the selected version. This avoids allowing one strong promotional photograph to lend qualities to another variant whose shape or construction has not been shown clearly.",
        "Give each paused candidate an expiry point. Recheck it only when a missing measurement appears, the option changes or the wardrobe role becomes more important. Reopening the same incomplete page repeatedly does not create evidence. Archiving it protects attention for better documented choices and keeps the shortlist small enough to use on mobile, where subtle differences between many tabs are especially easy to confuse."
        ,"If a candidate survives, write one sentence connecting construction, fit and outfit role: for example, the measured shape is compatible with the reference pair, the visible closure supports adjustment and the silhouette works with three owned bottoms. Every clause should point to recorded evidence. If the sentence relies on soft, premium, supportive or similar unsupported language, revise it. Clear decision language exposes exactly where marketing vocabulary has replaced a fact or personal requirement."
        ,"Archive the rejected rows with one short reason. They can prevent the same weak evidence from returning under a changed image or title later."
      ]]
    ],
    steps: ["Name the missing sneaker role", "Record fit and construction evidence", "Test owned-outfit proportions", "Pass, pause or remove each option"],
    sourceNote: "Independent wardrobe and visual-evidence method reviewed September 15, 2026. Listing images can inform questions but cannot prove long-wear comfort."
  },
  "hacoo-hoodies-sweaters-layering": {
    kicker: "Hacoo hoodies and sweaters / layering plan",
    dateLabel: `Published and reviewed ${reviewDate}`,
    intro: "Hacoo hoodies and sweaters are easiest to compare after you decide where the layer sits: against the body, over a T-shirt, under a jacket or as the outermost piece. The same chest width can feel and look different when fabric thickness, shoulder shape, hem structure and intended layers change. This guide begins with a garment you already wear, builds the required ease around a real outfit and separates visible construction from material or warmth claims that a listing does not prove.",
    factBox: [["Layer role", "Base, middle or outer layer"], ["Reference", "A similar garment worn in the intended outfit"], ["Stop condition", "Missing decisive measurement or unclear chart method"]],
    sections: [
      ["Choose the layer role first", [
        "Describe the complete outfit and weather rather than writing that you want a hoodie. A fitted zip layer under a coat has different requirements from an oversized pullover used instead of a jacket. Name the T-shirt, shirt or knit worn underneath and the outerwear, if any, worn above. The candidate must fit into that stack without turning every measurement into a separate guess. This also reveals whether the wardrobe needs warmth, easy removal, color balance or simply a different silhouette.",
        "Check for duplication. Lay out the hoodies and sweaters already serving the same temperature and occasion. Record why one is used and another is ignored: sleeve restriction, short body, bulky hood, difficult care or weak outfit coverage. The shortlist should solve one repeated limitation. A different print on the same unusable cut does not create a new role, and a lower displayed price does not make duplicate function more valuable."
      ]],
      ["Measure a reference in the intended stack", [
        "Choose an owned garment with the fit you want and measure shoulder, flat chest, sleeve, body length, cuff and hem. If the candidate will sit under a jacket, measure the reference while thinking about hood bulk, sleeve volume and how the hem behaves when layered. If it will go over a shirt, use a reference that already allows that room. Measurements copied from an unrelated thin top will create false confidence about a thick middle layer.",
        "Identify the chart method before comparing. Flat chest is not full circumference, raglan sleeves may start at the neck, and dropped shoulders make a conventional shoulder number less predictive. Look for a measurement diagram or photographed tape path. Keep everything in one unit and record unstated fields as unknown. Do not infer a missing measurement from a model image, because styling, pose and camera distance can hide the proportions most relevant to layering."
      ]],
      ["Read fabric information with clear limits", [
        "Separate stated composition, stated fabric weight and visible surface. A photograph may show a brushed interior, rib knit or smooth face, but it cannot prove fibre content, warmth, breathability or long-term pilling. If those qualities decide the layer's job, require current specifications or keep the point unresolved. Marketing words such as premium, heavy or soft need measurable context before they influence the comparison.",
        "Construction still offers useful evidence. Inspect neckline, hood panels, drawcord exits, shoulder joins, pocket attachment, cuffs and hem. For sweaters, review collar, shoulder shaping, knit density and seam transitions. Ask where a bag strap, jacket cuff or repeated movement creates stress. Describe what is visible without diagnosing durability. A neat close-up supports an observation about the pictured finish; it does not guarantee every unit or future wear."
      ]],
      ["Test movement and outerwear compatibility", [
        "Use the reference garment to perform the movements the outfit requires: reach forward, sit, lift arms and close the intended jacket. Note where fabric pulls or bunches. Then compare the candidate's decisive dimensions and silhouette. A wider chest with narrow cuffs may still feel restrictive; extra body length may interfere with a cropped outer layer. Layer planning works when measurements are interpreted as a system rather than ranked independently.",
        "Hood design deserves its own check when another jacket is involved. A thick or structured hood can crowd a collar, while a small hood may sit more easily but provide less coverage. Photographs can show panel count and relative bulk, not the final feel. If the hoodie will usually be worn under outerwear, prioritize compatibility over how the hood looks when styled alone. The real outfit, not the isolated product image, owns the decision."
      ]],
      ["Compare outfit coverage and care reality", [
        "Place each candidate against three owned bottoms and the shoes normally worn with them. Consider body length, hem width and color blocks. A long relaxed hoodie may work with narrow trousers and compete with a wide silhouette; a cropped sweater may need a rise you do not own. These are not fixed fashion rules. The purpose is to expose dependencies before purchase, especially when a saved look depends on proportions different from your wardrobe.",
        "Read any current care information and compare it with your routine. A light knit that requires delicate handling may not suit a high-frequency campus or commute role. Dark fleece that releases lint may interact poorly with the planned layer below. Do not invent shrinkage or wash performance. Mark care as supported, uncertain or incompatible, and let that status affect expected rotation. A practical layer is one you can wear and maintain repeatedly."
      ]],
      ["Finish with a layer-specific decision record", [
        "Use columns for role, underneath, outerwear, decisive measurements, construction evidence, care, three outfits and unknowns. Limit the final comparison to three options. Pass a candidate only when its evidence supports the full stack; pause it when one answer could change the result; remove it when the proportions or role conflict with the plan. A numeric score can hide a critical sleeve or length problem, so keep conditions visible in words.",
        "Open the exact live option after the matrix is complete. Confirm color, size, chart and gallery, because selectors may change what is displayed. Save the date and do not treat continued availability as guaranteed. The conclusion should be modest: the candidate fits the planned evidence range for a defined layer role. That is enough to make a better shortlist without pretending that an independent guide can promise warmth, hand feel or perfect fit.",
        "Check how the candidate changes the rest of the stack. A longer hoodie may require a longer jacket; a bulky sweater may reduce bag comfort; a high collar may compete with outerwear. Count these dependencies as part of the decision instead of treating the layer as isolated. An option with slightly less visual impact can be more useful when it works inside several existing stacks without demanding additional changes.",
        "After wear, update the reference rather than publishing a universal verdict. Record which underneath layer was used, the conditions, movement and any measurement difference from the chart. That private feedback helps the next shortlist because it connects fit language to your wardrobe. One delivered item cannot prove how every batch or option behaves, so keep the lesson tied to the exact garment and date."
        ,"Use sleeve interaction as a final tiebreaker. Check whether the middle layer's cuff fits comfortably beneath the planned jacket, whether fabric bunches at the elbow and whether pushing sleeves up is part of normal wear. A product gallery may show the cuff clearly but cannot demonstrate the stack in motion. The reference garment supplies that context, and the candidate measurements show whether the comparison is plausible enough to continue."
      ]]
    ],
    steps: ["Name the layer stack", "Measure a comparable garment", "Check construction, movement and care", "Confirm the exact live option"],
    sourceNote: "Independent layering and garment-comparison method reviewed against current hoodie and sweater routes on September 15, 2026."
  },
  "hacoo-t-shirts-fit-fabric-use": {
    kicker: "Hacoo T-shirts / fit and fabric role",
    dateLabel: `Published and reviewed ${reviewDate}`,
    intro: "A Hacoo T-shirt shortlist becomes useful when each candidate has a specific silhouette, fabric role and place in an existing rotation. Small differences at the shoulder, chest, sleeve and body length can change how a tee works under layers or with different rises. Product images can suggest shape and construction, but they cannot establish feel, shrinkage or long-term performance. This guide uses an owned reference, consistent measurements and realistic outfit coverage to reduce guesswork without turning one shirt into a universal recommendation.",
    factBox: [["Fit anchor", "One tee with the target silhouette"], ["Core measures", "Shoulder, chest, sleeve, body length and hem"], ["Wardrobe test", "Three bottoms plus one planned layer"]],
    sections: [
      ["Define the silhouette in useful terms", [
        "Replace broad labels such as oversized or regular with visible relationships. Decide where the shoulder should fall, how much room the chest needs, where the sleeve should end and how the hem should meet your usual waistband. Add whether the tee will be tucked, untucked or layered. A shirt intended beneath a close jacket needs different sleeve and body volume from one worn alone with wide trousers. The silhouette is a set of proportions, not one size word.",
        "Choose a reference tee you actually wear in that role. Photograph it flat and on the body if helpful, then record why it works. Perhaps the shoulder sits slightly beyond the joint, the sleeve reaches mid-arm and the hem remains below a high-rise waistband when moving. Those observations connect measurements to real use. Avoid selecting a sentimental favorite that has stretched or shrunk into a shape you cannot reliably reproduce."
      ]],
      ["Measure consistently before comparing charts", [
        "Lay the reference flat without pulling the fabric. Measure shoulder between seams when present, chest at the chart's stated point, sleeve from the same origin and length from the same neckline or shoulder point. Note whether chest is a flat width. Add hem width when taper matters. Use centimetres through the comparison and record the measurement method beside each number so a neat table does not disguise incompatible definitions.",
        "If the candidate uses dropped shoulders, raglan construction or a curved hem, the standard fields may need interpretation. Use the gallery to understand construction, then look for the closest comparable points. Do not estimate missing dimensions from the model's height or pose unless the listing provides enough context and the method is clear. A size letter that usually works for you is not evidence that these proportions match the reference."
      ]],
      ["Give fabric information a job", [
        "Decide what the fabric needs to do: sit smoothly under a sweater, hold a boxy shape, feel light in warm weather or provide an opaque base. Then separate stated composition, stated weight and visible drape. A photograph may show that the pictured tee falls softly or holds structure, but lighting, styling and sample size limit the conclusion. Do not infer cotton percentage, thickness or breathability from appearance alone.",
        "Inspect collar rib, neck tape, shoulder joins, sleeve hems, side seams and print placement. These details help compare the construction shown, especially when several options share a similar front image. They do not guarantee durability or identify hidden stitching. If collar recovery or print method is decisive and not described, write an explicit question or choose an option with clearer evidence. Missing information should narrow the shortlist, not invite a confident guess."
      ]],
      ["Match the tee to bottoms and layers", [
        "Test each candidate against three owned bottoms with different rises or volumes. Body length and hem width change whether a tee tucks cleanly, sits over a waistband or competes with a wide leg. Sleeve volume and shoulder position affect how it fits below an overshirt or jacket. Use outfit photographs and reference measurements together. A tee may look balanced alone and become uncomfortable or visually crowded inside the layer it is meant to support.",
        "Color and graphic scale also need a wardrobe role. A large central print may be hidden by an open jacket or dominate a simple rotation; a plain support color may connect more outfits. Neither is automatically better. Count the combinations you would genuinely wear, then note whether the candidate adds a new role or repeats an existing tee. Outfit coverage is stronger evidence of value than the number of images saved."
      ]],
      ["Plan for care without inventing outcomes", [
        "Read current care information where available and record it literally. Washing temperature, drying method and print care can affect whether the tee suits your routine. Do not publish or rely on an assumed shrink percentage. If a very precise fit would fail after even a small change, the available evidence may not support that narrow decision. A little margin can be more practical than chasing an exact chest number unsupported by tolerance information.",
        "Consider rotation frequency. A high-use base tee should be easy to combine and maintain; an occasional graphic tee can carry a narrower outfit role. Label expected wear weekly, monthly or occasional and explain the reason. This keeps an attractive but specialized shirt from being compared as if it serves the same purpose as a repeat base layer. Fit, fabric role and frequency should point in the same direction."
      ]],
      ["Use a transparent final shortlist", [
        "Create rows for no more than three tees. Record the exact option, reference differences, fabric evidence, collar and seam observations, outfit count, layer compatibility, care and unresolved points. Use pass, pause or remove. A missing length may pause a tuck-dependent option even when every other field looks strong. This conditional language prevents a total score from washing out the one detail that controls the use case.",
        "Reopen the live destination and verify the selector, chart, color and gallery before deciding. Save a dated screenshot of the exact option, not just the cover image. If information has changed, update the row rather than protecting the earlier conclusion. The guide's job is to make the comparison repeatable. It cannot promise hand feel or final fit, but it can ensure that the shortlist reflects the silhouette and wardrobe role you intended.",
        "Check the neckline against the planned use. A high rib can remain visible under an overshirt, a wide opening may expose a base layer and a heavy collar can affect how a knit sits above it. Use clear front and interior images when available, and keep recovery or softness unknown without evidence. Collar shape often influences repeat wear more than a graphic that first attracted attention.",
        "If two tees pass, choose the one that improves the rotation rather than duplicating the reference perfectly. It may add a useful support color, work better with one jacket or provide a distinct warmer-weather silhouette. Write that improvement in one sentence. If no sentence is possible, the shortlist has identified a preference, not a gap, and waiting remains a valid outcome."
        ,"Keep print and decoration evidence tied to the selected size. Placement can appear different when the garment proportions change, and promotional close-ups can hide overall scale. Look for a full front view, a detail view and an option name that clearly belong together. If the graphic is the main reason for the choice but the exact version is ambiguous, pause it. Decoration should not receive a lower evidence standard simply because it does not affect physical fit."
        ,"Review transparency and layering together when a light color is shortlisted. Use stated fabric information and several current views; do not infer opacity from one studio background."
      ]]
    ],
    steps: ["Describe the target silhouette", "Measure an owned reference tee", "Test fabric role, bottoms and layers", "Verify the live option and chart"],
    sourceNote: "Independent fit and wardrobe-role method reviewed against the current T-shirt category route on September 15, 2026."
  },
  "hacoo-jackets-season-layering": {
    kicker: "Hacoo jackets / season and layering",
    dateLabel: `Published and reviewed ${reviewDate}`,
    intro: "The right Hacoo jacket candidate is the one whose climate job, layer allowance and construction evidence match the days you will actually wear it. A jacket can look versatile in isolation while duplicating outerwear you own, failing over a usual hoodie or offering no reliable information about closure and lining. This guide builds the brief before the shortlist. It uses current images and measurements carefully and leaves weather protection, warmth and performance unknown unless the listing provides evidence that supports those claims.",
    factBox: [["Climate job", "Temperature context, wind or rain exposure and time outside"], ["Fit reference", "An owned jacket used with the same layers"], ["Critical checks", "Closure, pockets, lining, cuffs, hem and movement room"]],
    sections: [
      ["Describe the season as conditions, not a label", [
        "Spring, autumn and winter mean different things by location and routine. Write the expected temperature range only if you know it, then add wind, rain exposure, time outdoors and activity. A ten-minute walk between buildings requires a different layer from long stationary waits. The brief should also state whether an umbrella, bag or warm middle layer is normally present. Conditions make the comparison useful without asking a product image to certify technical performance.",
        "Name the jacket's place in the current outerwear lineup. It may replace a damaged shell, bridge the gap between overshirt and coat, or add a smarter layer for casual work. Lay out nearby alternatives and identify what they fail to do. If an owned jacket already covers the same conditions and outfits, a new color alone may be a preference rather than a gap. That is acceptable when labeled honestly, but it should not receive the same priority as a repeated practical need."
      ]],
      ["Measure the full planned layer stack", [
        "Choose an owned jacket with the desired room and wear it over the thickest normal middle layer. Measure flat chest, shoulder where applicable, sleeve and body length, then note movement at the back, armhole and elbow. Compare the candidate chart using the same points. External garment measurements do not directly describe internal space because lining, insulation and construction consume room, so avoid treating a small numerical difference as a precise fit guarantee.",
        "Closure matters to fit. A jacket that looks comfortable open may pull when zipped, and a cropped hem may lift during movement. Review front and side images with the closure in the intended state. Check cuff adjustment and hem shape when wind or layering is part of the brief. If the listing does not show a closed view or decisive measurement, mark the option unresolved instead of using a styled open image as evidence."
      ]],
      ["Inspect construction by its actual job", [
        "Map the features needed for the conditions: usable closure, secure pockets, collar or hood geometry, cuff control and enough coverage at the hem. Then inspect what the current gallery actually shows. Look at zipper path, snaps, pocket openings, lining joins and stress points around shoulders. Describe the pictured construction, not an assumed performance level. A hood does not prove rain protection, and a thick appearance does not establish warmth.",
        "Treat material and weather language carefully. Stated composition can be recorded; waterproof, breathable or insulated performance needs reliable current specifications. If the jacket's job depends on those qualities and the evidence is absent, the candidate should not pass merely because the silhouette resembles technical outerwear. A responsible Hacoo jacket shortlist distinguishes visual styling from functional proof and chooses a different candidate when the gap matters."
      ]],
      ["Check pockets, bags and daily movement", [
        "List what the jacket must carry and whether you use a shoulder bag or backpack. Pocket placement can interfere with straps, seated posture or a waistband. Gallery images should show openings and closures clearly enough to understand access, but they may not prove capacity. If secure storage is essential, require dimensions or a clear interior view rather than assuming that a visible flap conceals a usable zipped pocket.",
        "Perform reach, sit and carry tests in the reference jacket. Note sleeve ride, back tension, hood movement and where the bag strap presses. Translate those observations into candidate questions. A dropped shoulder may provide visual room yet bunch under a narrow backpack strap; a short rigid hem may suit standing and feel different when seated. The use case decides which tradeoff matters."
      ]],
      ["Test the jacket across existing outfits", [
        "Use three complete outfits representing the planned conditions. Include the bulkiest middle layer, the most common trousers and both narrow and larger shoe silhouettes if they are in rotation. Compare jacket length, shoulder volume, color and closure lines. A candidate that works only in the styled listing outfit may create more wardrobe problems than it solves. Outfit testing turns broad versatility into visible, personal evidence.",
        "Check whether the color and silhouette duplicate another jacket. Two dark short layers can serve different jobs when one handles wet conditions and the other is a lightweight indoor-to-outdoor piece, but the distinction must be supported. If functional evidence is missing, they may simply compete. Give preference to the candidate with a clearer role and more complete measurements rather than the one with the most dramatic photograph."
      ]],
      ["Finish with conditions for pass or pause", [
        "Build a matrix for climate job, layer fit, closure, pockets, lining, cuffs, hem, outfits, care and unknowns. Pass means the available evidence is sufficient for your defined role; pause means one answer could change the result; remove means the option conflicts with a decisive condition. Keep warmth and weather protection marked unknown unless supported. This makes the conclusion useful without implying an independent test occurred.",
        "At the live destination, confirm exact size, color, chart and current terms. Save the selected option and date. If the gallery or chart changes, revise the evidence status. The strongest conclusion is deliberately narrow: this jacket is a plausible candidate for these layers and conditions based on these visible and stated details. That is more trustworthy—and more actionable—than naming a universal best Hacoo jacket.",
        "Add storage and care to the decision. Structured jackets, coated surfaces and bulky hoods may need more space or a different cleaning routine than a light overshirt. Use current care information and avoid guessing from appearance. A jacket expected to handle frequent use but difficult to store or maintain may rotate less than planned, which changes its value even when the fit and color are strong.",
        "Review the candidate with the jacket closed, open and carried. Many days include all three states. Check whether the interior or reverse side is shown clearly enough for open wear, whether the shape remains useful when carried and whether a bag can be used without blocking essential pockets. This small scenario test turns a static product image into a more complete wardrobe decision without claiming unobserved performance."
        ,"Create one fallback outfit for unexpected conditions. If the temperature rises, can the jacket be carried or worn open without making the rest of the outfit unusable? If wind increases, does the brief depend on a closure or cuff feature the page actually shows? The fallback does not prove protection; it reveals whether the wardrobe plan has alternatives. A candidate that works only in one narrow styled state may deserve a more limited rotation label."
        ,"Keep one non-negotiable condition visible at the top of the record. This prevents attractive secondary details from gradually replacing the climate or layering requirement that began the search."
      ]]
    ],
    steps: ["Define climate and rotation job", "Measure the real layer stack", "Inspect functional construction", "Pass or pause the exact live option"],
    sourceNote: "Independent season, layering and construction method reviewed against the current jacket route on September 15, 2026; no technical performance is inferred from styling alone."
  },
  "hacoo-pants-shorts-proportions": {
    kicker: "Hacoo pants and shorts / proportion plan",
    dateLabel: `Published and reviewed ${reviewDate}`,
    intro: "Hacoo pants and shorts need more than a waist label. Rise changes where the garment sits, inseam changes where it ends, thigh and opening control movement and silhouette, and each of those choices interacts with the tops and shoes already in your wardrobe. This guide creates a fit reference from a pair you wear, checks the chart method and tests full-outfit proportions. It reduces avoidable guesses while keeping fabric feel, stretch and final fit appropriately uncertain when the current evidence is incomplete.",
    factBox: [["Core measures", "Waist method, rise, thigh, inseam and opening"], ["Use test", "Sitting, walking and the planned shoe"], ["Comparison rule", "Match measurement method before matching numbers"]],
    sections: [
      ["Start with occasion and movement", [
        "Describe where the garment will be worn and what the day asks of it: long sitting, cycling, walking, warm weather, casual work or travel. Add what must fit in the pockets and whether a belt is normally used. Movement changes which measurements are decisive. A short designed for standing photographs may need more rise or thigh room for seated use, while a trouser for walking may depend on opening and fabric behavior as much as waist.",
        "Choose one owned pair used for the same occasion and record what works or fails. The waistband may stay comfortable while seated, the rise may place tops well, or the opening may balance a larger sneaker. An avoided pair is equally useful if you can name the problem. These references keep the shortlist grounded in real proportions rather than a generic fit word taken from the listing."
      ]],
      ["Identify the waistband method", [
        "Waist figures may describe a flat garment width, full circumference, body measurement or relaxed-to-stretched elastic range. Determine the method before comparing. Measure the owned pair in the same state—fastened, flat and without pulling—then record whether the result needs doubling. Do not compare your body circumference directly with a flat garment value or assume an elastic maximum describes comfortable everyday tension.",
        "Review closure and adjustment. Buttons, hooks, zips, drawstrings, elastic panels and belt loops affect how a similar number behaves. Product images can show the system, while the description may provide range or material information. Neither proves comfort. If the waist method is missing or conflicting, pause the candidate. Averaging incompatible numbers produces precision without meaning and is especially risky when the intended fit has little margin."
      ]],
      ["Use rise and inseam to locate the garment", [
        "Front and back rise influence where the waistband sits and how the garment feels when seated. Inseam begins from that structure, so two pairs with the same inseam can end at different places on the body. Measure the reference using the chart's points and note intended waist position. For shorts, include outseam or total length when available because inseam alone does not fully describe the visual line.",
        "Do not infer rise from a model pose. Styling can pull a waistband higher or lower, and image cropping removes the context needed for comparison. Look for flat garment measurements and clear front and back views. If the rise controls how the item works with cropped or tucked tops, make it a stop condition. A familiar waist size cannot compensate for an unknown vertical proportion."
      ]],
      ["Compare thigh, knee and opening", [
        "Thigh width affects movement and how fabric falls from the seat. Knee and leg opening define taper, straightness or volume through the lower half. Measure the reference at equivalent points, because a thigh value taken at the crotch cannot be compared with one taken several centimetres below. Add fabric stretch only when the listing states it clearly; a photograph of a soft drape does not measure elasticity.",
        "For shorts, opening and hem structure affect seated comfort and movement. For trousers, opening affects the break over shoes. A narrow opening may sit above a larger sneaker; a wide opening may cover it and change apparent length. These effects are personal and outfit-dependent. Test them with the actual shoe silhouettes in your rotation instead of treating slim, straight or wide as universal recommendations."
      ]],
      ["Build full outfits around the proportions", [
        "Pair each candidate with three owned tops: one normally tucked, one untucked and one layer. Compare waistband position, rise, body length interaction and overall volume. Then add the shoes most likely to be worn. A trouser that works with one cropped top but conflicts with every normal-length layer has narrow coverage. A short that balances several tops and shoes may earn more rotation even when it looks simpler alone.",
        "Color and fabric appearance should support the role, not distract from it. Mark whether a finish appears structured, flowing or textured in the current images, but do not infer composition, opacity or durability without evidence. If wrinkle behavior or stretch is decisive for work or travel, require current information. The wardrobe matrix should show both outfit compatibility and unresolved material questions."
      ]],
      ["Make a movement-aware final decision", [
        "Use rows for exact option, waistband method, rise, thigh, inseam, opening, closure, movement need, three outfits, shoes and unknowns. Pass only when the chart method and decisive proportions are comparable. Pause when one missing measurement could change fit. Remove when the evidence conflicts with the intended use. Avoid one total score: a missing rise can outweigh several visual matches for someone whose main requirement is seated comfort.",
        "Recheck the live selector because color and size changes can load different charts or images. Save the current option and date, then review the destination terms. The record cannot guarantee fit or movement, but it can show why the candidate is reasonable for a specific body reference and wardrobe. That transparent link between measurements, movement and outfit proportion is the practical value of a Hacoo pants and shorts guide.",
        "Include pocket evidence when the use case depends on it. Count visible pockets, distinguish real openings from decorative seams and look for closure or interior views. Photographs rarely prove capacity, so list the objects that must be carried and keep the result uncertain when scale is unclear. A pair intended for travel or phone-free hands can fail its wardrobe job even when the waist and leg proportions are well documented.",
        "Check the rear view as carefully as the front. Yoke, pocket placement, rise and seam shape influence both fit interpretation and how tops sit over the garment. A single front pose is insufficient when the decision depends on seated comfort or full silhouette. Ask for comparable flat or neutral views rather than using a stretched model pose to fill the evidence gap.",
        "After delivery, compare the garment with the saved chart using the same method before drawing conclusions. Record selected option, actual flat measurements and the movement context privately. Do not generalize one sample to every color, size or later batch. The useful feedback is a dated relationship between this item and your reference, which can improve the next shortlist without becoming a universal sizing claim."
        ,"For shorts, repeat the outfit test while seated because hem opening and rise can change more visibly than they do when standing. For trousers, test the planned shoe while walking and seated to understand break and cuff behavior. Online evidence cannot reproduce those experiences, but the shortlist can prioritize measurements linked to them. This keeps styling analysis connected to movement instead of treating a front-facing image as the complete silhouette."
      ]]
    ],
    steps: ["Define occasion and movement", "Match waistband and chart methods", "Compare rise, leg and shoe proportions", "Recheck the exact live option"],
    sourceNote: "Independent measurement and proportion method reviewed against the current pants and shorts route on September 15, 2026."
  },
};

